\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The joint gauge and real-matter measure}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Fix the graph, positive edge weights and invariant fiber metric of
a \vocabk{connection background}{fdip-006D}. When using a Wilson action,
also supply \vocabk{square faces and their attaching walks}{fdip-006M}. Let #{G} be a finite
group, or a compact Hausdorff group supplied with a normalized
bi-invariant Borel probability measure #{\mu_G}. In the finite case
use uniform probability measure. Let #{\rho:G\to O(F)} be a
continuous orthogonal representation on a finite-dimensional real
inner-product space; continuity is automatic for a finite group.
The compact-group measure is assumed as part of the data here.}

\p{Put #{\mathcal H=F^V}, #{N=\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathcal H} and
#{d\mu(U)=\prod_{e\in E}d\mu_G(U_e)}. Choose #{m>0} and write
#{Q_U=L_U+m^2\bbI}. Use a continuous real gauge-invariant action
#{S_W(U)}, such as the \vocabk{Wilson action}{fdip-006O} with its declared
representation and coupling. With ordinary Lebesgue volume
#{d\operatorname{vol}(\phi)} in the fixed metric, define
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathfrak Z
 &=\int_{G^E}\int_{\mathcal H}
 \ue^{-S_W(U)-\frac12\langle\phi,Q_U\phi\rangle}
 \,d\operatorname{vol}(\phi)\,d\mu(U),\\
d\mathbb P(U,\phi)
 &=\mathfrak Z^{-1}
 \ue^{-S_W(U)-\frac12\langle\phi,Q_U\phi\rangle}
 \,d\mu(U)\,d\operatorname{vol}(\phi).
\end{aligned}}
This is a law on both the connection and the real matter field.}

\p{The normalization is finite and positive. Indeed,
#{Q_U\ge m^2\bbI}, and the continuous action has a finite lower bound
on compact #{G^E}. Thus the integrand is bounded by a constant times
#{\ue^{-m^2\|\phi\|^2/2}}, which is integrable. It is everywhere
positive. A gauge transformation preserves the edge measure by left
and right invariance, the matter volume by orthogonality, and the
quadratic form by [gauge covariance](fdip-006E). Hence the joint law
is gauge invariant.}

\p{The edge measure differs from the unnormalized counting convention
in \ref{fdip-006O}: for finite #{G}, replacing #{\mu} by counting
measure multiplies #{\mathfrak Z} by #{|G|^{|E|}} and leaves
normalized expectations unchanged. The matter volume is also explicit:
it has not been divided by #{(2\pi)^{N/2}}.}
